    </div>
    <p>OSWorld here is version 2.1 on the offline subset, not the OSWorld 2.0 prints on the Opus 5.5 and Fable 5.1 pages. Anthropic’s announce charts also show Haiku 5.5 scores and cost at each effort level on OSWorld, GDPval-AA, and Humanity’s Last Exam.</p>
    <p>Artificial Analysis measured about 243 output tokens per second at max effort (<a href="https://artificialanalysis.ai/models/claude-haiku-5-5">AA Haiku 5.5 model page</a>, fetched 8 Oct 2026).</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Hardware</h2>
    <p>Anthropic has not said which chips train or serve Haiku 5.5. Across the company, it trains and runs Claude on AWS Trainium, Google TPUs, and NVIDIA GPUs (<a href="https://www.anthropic.com/news/google-broadcom-partnership-compute">Anthropic, Google and Broadcom compute deal</a>). Its Google TPU deals are on the <a href="/ai/hardware/chips/ironwood/">Ironwood</a> page.</p>
    <h2 class="ai-model-section">Access and pricing</h2>
    <div class="ai-model-table-wrap">
      <table class="ai-model-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col"></th>
            <th scope="col">Haiku 5.5, prompts up to 100K</th>
            <th scope="col">Haiku 5.5, prompts over 100K</th>
            <th scope="col">Haiku 4.5</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Input / MTok</td><td>$0.10</td><td>$0.50</td><td>$1</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Output / MTok</td><td>$0.50</td><td>$2.50</td><td>$5</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cache reads / MTok</td><td>$0.01</td><td>$0.05</td><td>$0.10</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cache writes, 5 minutes / MTok</td><td>$0.125</td><td>$0.625</td><td>$1.25</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cache writes, 1 hour / MTok</td><td>$0.20</td><td>$1</td><td>-</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>The price depends on prompt length. Prompts up to 100,000 tokens get the low rate. Longer prompts cost five times as much per token. Cache reads are the cheap rate for reusing a prompt the model has already seen. The Batch API is 50% off these prices.</p>
    <p>The list price is 90% below Haiku 4.5 on prompts up to 100,000 tokens. Anthropic says those short prompts made up about 90% of Haiku 4.5 requests. Above 100,000 tokens, the cut is only 50%. Add the new tokenizer, which counts the same text as about 30% more tokens, and Anthropic’s own average comes out at "around 75% less to run" than Haiku 4.5, not 90%.</p>
    <p>Artificial Analysis shows the other half of the story, in figures fetched 8 Oct 2026. Running its full test set cost $0.21 per task on Haiku 5.5 at max effort, against $0.07 for GPT-6 Luna at the same list price. The reason is length. Haiku 5.5 wrote 440 million output tokens across the tests, against a median of 100 million for models in its class. The same sticker price does not mean the same bill. What you pay is price per token times the tokens a model actually writes.</p>
    <p>Available on the Claude API, Amazon Bedrock (<code>anthropic.claude-haiku-5-5</code>), Google Cloud, Microsoft Foundry, and Claude Platform on AWS. In Claude Code, the <code>haiku</code> shortcut points to Haiku 5.5 only on the Anthropic API, and you need Claude Code v2.1.293 or later. On Claude Platform on AWS, Bedrock, Google Cloud, and Foundry, the shortcut still points to Haiku 4.5, so you pick <code>claude-haiku-5-5</code> by name. Anthropic will not retire the model before 7 October 2027.</p>
    <p>In the same launch Anthropic halved cache reads on <a href="/ai/models/anthropic/claude-sonnet-5-5/">Claude Sonnet 5.5</a>, from $0.20 to $0.10 per million tokens. It says that makes Sonnet 5.5 about 20% cheaper on most agent work. Anthropic also added a monthly API credit: $100 for Max 5x, $200 for Max 20x, and up to $500 pooled for Team plans.</p>
